  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/b/bc/Tales_of_Suspense_Vol_1_48.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "When a steel supplier named Mr. Carter suddenly backs out of a deal with Tony Stark, he drives over to investigate, only to discover that Carter is being held captive by a \"Mister Doll,\" who is coercing him to sign over his company to his captor.\n\nStark attacks as Iron Man, but the mysterious Mister Doll quickly changes the shape of the doll he's holding to look like Iron Man and begins to crush him, making Tony feel it inside his armor.&nbsp; He has no choice but to retreat and actually falls off the balcony into water below.&nbsp; Thinking that his enemy is defeated, Mr. Doll refashions the head of the doll to take the form of Carter once again and continues to threaten the terrified man.\n\nBut the change in appearance of the doll has also released his control over Iron Man, who drags himself out of the water.&nbsp; Needing a charge to keep his heart pumping, he barely makes it back to his office in time to plug in.\n\nTony realizes that he needs recharging too often and blames the ponderous golden suit, which is too heavy and saps too much power.&nbsp; He'll have to design a lighter version so that it won't be such a strain on his heart.&nbsp; He soon designs a sleeker, red and gold version and demonstrates its new abilities, including the fact that, being lighter, he can pack more gizmos into it!&nbsp; And his headgear can now show more expression.\n\nMeanwhile, the local police precinct calls in Mr. Carter to urge him to file a complaint.&nbsp; There simply <i>has</i> to be a reason that he is the third millionaire Mr. Doll has forced into signing over his fortune.&nbsp; The police are <i>baffled</i> as to why this is happening and vow to figure out who is <i>next</i> on Mr. Doll's list.\n\nAs the fourth richest man in the city, it turns out that Tony Stark is Mr. Doll's next target!&nbsp; The police recognize the pattern and go to warn Stark, who agrees to be a decoy so they can catch Mr. Doll's in the act.\n\nPretending to be interested in Pepper to \"ask her for a date,\" and asking to be alone with her, the surprised woman soon finds that Tony did this only as a ruse to shake the police guard.&nbsp; Tony quickly slips away through a secret exit on the pretext that he's off to find Iron Man, leaving Pepper alone to sob in disappointment\n\nSure enough, Mr. Doll soon appears, and the new Iron Man stands, ready to face him.&nbsp; But Doll is ready, alters the doll's features to resemble Iron Man, and begins to twist his doll's arm, which soon causes Stark intense pain.&nbsp; Yet, because the new armor is lighter, the strain on his heart is therefore lessened, and he's able to withstand the pain better.&nbsp; He <i>pretends</i> to succumb to Doll's will so that he'll think he now controls Iron Man.\n\nMr. Doll reveals that the doll came from a witch doctor in Africa, and, believing that Iron Man has been frightened enough in the face of his awesome power, he refashions it into Tony Stark's image in order to force the weapons manufacturer to come to <i>him</i>.&nbsp; This causes Iron Man to turn away in agony, though not willing to let it show for fear of his dual identity being exposed.\n\nPretending to flee in fear, Iron Man leaves the scene and locks himself in a lab to prevent himself from returning, and even as the pain increases, Tony Stark puts his genius to work and manages to fashion the only counter weapon he feels will be effective.\n\nWhen he next confronts Mr. Doll as Iron Man, he wields the weapon, which is a transistor-powered gadget that literally refashions the voodoo doll while still in the hands of its operator.&nbsp; Iron Man quickly reshapes it into the form of Mr. Doll himself, which surprises the villain enough for him to drop it.&nbsp; And in seconds, he too crumbles to the ground, totally defeated!\n\nAfter Tony reappears, he and Happy realize that Pepper is still in the sealed room all by herself, and she is so incensed that she storms off as they watch on in bafflement.",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME -- write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME -- write something",
  "review_text": "Mister Doll seems like a cross between the Puppet Master and a bokor (sometimes spelled bocor), a sort of sorcerer in Haitian Vodou.&nbsp; In fact, voodoo is <i>exactly</i> the sort of vibe that this guy gives off, particularly after he refashions his doll in the shape of Iron Man's helmet.\n[ASSET: center | MisterDollsPower | width=67%]\n\n[ASSET: left | ItsTheIronSuit | width=12%] Though Stark slips away from his antagonist, the encounter opens up his eyes, revealing that his ponderous iron suit is to blame for much of the power shortages he experiences on a regular basis.&nbsp; The constant need to recharge it has brought him close to the brink of death on multiple occasions.\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | ArmorFinished | width=16%] [ASSET: right | TheNewIronMan | width=30%] There <i>has</i> to be a way to redesign his armor to make it lighter in weight without sacrificing strength.\n[BR]\n[BR] After working in his lab for several hours, pages 8 through 10 gradually reveal a new, sleeker and lighter-weight armor (and are certainly the highlight of the story), with artist Steve Ditko slowly rolling out what the new armor can do (though we've seen Don Heck <i>also</i> fully capable of highlighting the armor's gadgetry).\n[BR]\n[BR] The new-look Iron Man stands fully-revealed at the end of page 10, ready to go into action!\n\nWhile the new armor is interesting, page 12 shows that Tony isn't above manipulation, inadvertently playing with Pepper's emotions, all with the motive of shaking his police guard so that he could change to Iron Man and be ready for Mr. Doll when he eventually arrived.\n\n[ASSET: right | PepperThrowsHerselfAtStark | width=14%] Happy and Pepper already had a one-panel bickering session on about every other page, which is apparently supposed to be the \"charm\" of the book.&nbsp; But I found it quickly growing tiresome.\n[BR]\n[BR] And the moment when Pepper throws herself at her boss was, by <i>far</i>, the lowpoint of the book,  as it reduces her to little more than a romantic foil with hardly any personality above \"Oh, my employer <i>loves </i>me!&nbsp; Life is <i>wonderful</i>!\"&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And then she learns it was all for show on the part of Stark)]\n\nI'm sorry, but [SPAN: severity=error; text=this isn't character development.&nbsp; This is nothing less than forcing a supporting character to punch in for the month], and <i>honestly</i>, this book needs a love triangle about as much as Iron Man needs an extra helmet!\n\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\nWere it not for the updated armor (apparently designed by guest-artist Steve Ditko), this would have been another run of the mill \"reader\" story.\n\n\nAll of that, at least, was well-done, with my only complaint being that the suit has a more painted-on look to it.&nbsp; It doesn't seem like armor at all when Ditko draws it, so I hope this was a more one-off guest stint for him, at least affording him the option of showcasing the new look he designed.\n\nHowever, the villain was silly and largely unimaginative.\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nAgain, were it not that this is the first appearance of the long-time look of Iron Man (albeit with the pointed head gear, which is later rounded out), this one would be soon forgotten.\n\n[DEVNOTE: Mr. Doll later becomes Brother Grimm, or Brothers Grimm, as seen in Spider-Woman in the 1970s]",
  "ownership": "I got a reader-grade copy as part of the 2000 collection, for which I paid a little over $23, which sold on eBay in Jan, 2021 for $169, netting me over $122.",
